<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manitoba Act</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> Act, passed by the Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in May 1870, created the province of Manitoba from part of Rupert's Land after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-resistance">Red River Resistance</a>. Negotiated with delegates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a>'s provisional government, it provided for French and English as official languages, separate Catholic and Protestant schools and a land grant for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> children. The new province was small, centred on the Red River settlement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manitoba-act.jpg" alt="Manitoba Act" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saffron Blaze · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centre_Block_-_Parliament_Hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
